iles are W9, T1, T8, T9 (two), F2, F4, J2. Maybe a pair of T9s. So the hand would be three chows (W2-W3-W4, W3-W4-W5, W4-W5-W6), a pair of T9s, and the remaining tiles (W9, T1, T8, F2, F4, J2). Wait, that doesn't add up. Wait, the total tiles would be 3 chows (each 3 tiles) is 9 tiles, plus a pair (2 tiles) = 11 tiles. But I have 14 tiles. So maybe I have another meld. Hmm, maybe I'm missing something.\\n\\nAlternatively, if I have four chows. For example, W2-W3-W4, W3-W4-W5, W4-W5-W6, and maybe another chow. But I don't have enough tiles. Maybe the W9 and T1 are part of a different meld. Not sure.\\n\\nGiven that the valid actions are Peng T9 or Pass, I need to decide which is better. Penging T9 gives a Pung, which can contribute to All Pungs (4 points) if I can form four pungs. But I don't have enough pungs. Alternatively, the Pung of T9s could be part of a pattern like Two Pung of Terminals (2 points each). But with only one Pung, that's 2 points.\\n\\nAlternatively, if I don't Peng, maybe I can form a Pure Straight with the W2-W6 sequence. Let's see: three chows (W2-W3-W4, W3-W4-W5, W4-W5-W6) plus a pair. That would be a Pure Straight, which is 16 points. If that's possible, then not Penging and keeping the tiles to form that would be better. But I need to check if that's feasible.\\n\\nWait, to form a Pure Straight, you need three chows in the same suit. The example given is B1, B2, B3, B4, B5, B6, B7, B8, B9, T2, T3, T4, J2, J2. So in my case, if I have W2, W3, W4, W5, W6, that's five tiles. To form three chows, I need to split them into three sequences. For example:\\n\\n1. W2-W3-W4\\n2. W3-W4-W5\\n3. W4-W5-W6\\n\\nThat uses all five tiles (each tile is part of multiple chows). Then, the remaining tiles would be W9, T1, T8, T9 (two), F2, F4, J2. I need a fourth meld and a pair. The fourth meld could be a pair of T9s, but that's a pair, not a meld. Wait, the winning hand requires four melds and a pair. So if I have three chows (each 3 tiles) and a pair, that's