emaining tiles. \n\nAlternatively, playing W2 might help in forming another Chi. For example, if I have W2 and can Chi on a discarded tile. But since I'm the one playing, I need to think about my own hand. \n\nGiven the options, I think the best move is to play W6 to form the Chi of W4-W5-W6. This gives me two Chis in the W suit. Then I can focus on forming the remaining melds. The remaining tiles would be W2, W9, T8, B2, F4. Maybe a pair of W9 if I can get another one. But since there are three W9s left in the unshown tiles, it's possible. \n\nSo the answer is to play W6.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing W6 allows forming a third Chi in the W suit (W4-W5-W6), improving the hand structure towards Pure Triple Chow (24 points) or All Chows (2 points). Other plays like W2 or W4 may disrupt existing melds or reduce flexibility.\nReason: Forming a third Chi in the W suit (W4-W5-W6) creates a stronger hand with two Chis, aligning with high-value patterns. Remaining tiles (W2, W9, T8, B2, F4) can potentially form additional melds or pairs as the game progresses.\nAnswer: Play W6"}
